@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+---
+id: dl-006-refund-in-full-before-a-rental-starts-nothing-after
+type: decision-log
+title: "Refund in full before a rental starts, nothing after"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`cancel(bookingId, at)` must return `{ bookingId, refund }`. Unlike dl-001/dl-003's whole-day rule or
+dl-004's discount, this request does not state a refund schedule (no cancellation fee, no proration
+rule). dl-002 deliberately left cancellation and "any refund logic implementing [a cancellation policy]"
+out of scope; that policy was never written down anywhere this session can read, since each session only
+inherits the repository itself. `at` is passed in explicitly rather than read from the clock, consistent
+with the determinism directive and with how `book`/`quote` already take `start`/`end` as parameters
+instead of calling `Date.now()`.
+
+## Decision
+
+Take `at`'s relation to the booking's own period as the one signal available, rather than inventing a
+multi-tier fee schedule that wasn't asked for: refund the full `total` when `at` is strictly before the
+booking's `startMs`, and refund `0` once `at` is at or after `startMs` (the rental has begun, or is
+already over). This is the simplest rule that gives `at` an actual effect instead of being purely a log
+timestamp, and it mirrors a common real-world policy (cancel ahead of time, get your money back;
+cancelling once the gear is out the door does not undo that). Cancelling still always frees the
+equipment's booked period, regardless of `at` or the refund outcome, matching the request's "no longer
+holds its equipment" literally and unconditionally.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A customer who cancels any time before their rental starts is made whole; a customer who cancels during
+  or after it gets nothing back — there is no partial/prorated refund for a rental cancelled partway
+  through.
+- A booking's period and item are looked up via new `bookingPeriods`/`bookings` maps keyed by booking id
+  (the latter replacing the previous plain array), keeping `cancel` O(1) instead of scanning all bookings.
+- If the school later wants a different schedule (e.g. a cancellation fee, or a grace window after
+  booking), only the one-line `refund` computation in `cancel` changes; nothing else in `book`/`quote`/
+  `availability` depends on it.
+- A second `cancel` on the same booking throws rather than returning a stale or zero refund again, so
+  "cancelled once" is enforced at the API boundary, not left to the caller to avoid.
